WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:07.260
JAMES MALONE: Hi,
I'm James Malone.

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:09.840
Today, I'm here to talk about
what's new in open-source data

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:12.300
processing on Google Cloud.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:14.630
So, today, I want to
talk about six things.

00:00:14.630 --> 00:00:17.100
I want to talk about
who we are, and then I

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:19.410
want to talk about a
few key releases that

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:23.340
are really exciting for us,
Dataproc Metastore, Dataproc

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:26.400
Hub, some of our
open-source updates,

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:28.470
a few new features,
and then how you

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:31.560
can get started using our
managed open-source services

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:34.660
on Google Cloud.

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:37.690
First, I want to talk
about Open Data Analytics.

00:00:37.690 --> 00:00:40.150
We're a group that's
passionate about combining

00:00:40.150 --> 00:00:43.690
Google infrastructure with
the best of Google Cloud.

00:00:43.690 --> 00:00:46.640
We think that open source
represents, in many cases,

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:48.220
the best of software.

00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:49.780
It's community driven.

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:51.280
It moves quickly.

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:54.710
It really pushes the
envelope of what's possible.

00:00:54.710 --> 00:00:57.250
We also think that Google
Cloud can run infrastructure

00:00:57.250 --> 00:00:58.430
like nobody else.

00:00:58.430 --> 00:01:01.240
So we're really
interested in combining

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:04.569
the power of open source
and the power of Google's

00:01:04.569 --> 00:01:08.950
infrastructure to create managed
services so you can use managed

00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:11.080
open source on Google Cloud.

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:14.480
We think that open source
should be efficient, secure,

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:17.630
and accessible.

00:01:17.630 --> 00:01:21.770
Over the last year, we've
released over 40 releases.

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:23.870
And the reason I
bring this up is

00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:26.390
we are very interested
in keeping up

00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:30.110
with the open-source community
and all of the new releases,

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:32.540
but we also want our
customers to rapidly

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:35.510
have the latest and
greatest in both open source

00:01:35.510 --> 00:01:37.770
and, also, Google Cloud
platform features,

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:40.790
things like VPC
Service Controls.

00:01:40.790 --> 00:01:43.460
We think, the faster that
we can enable our customers,

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:46.760
the faster they have access to
the best of both open source

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:49.930
and Google Cloud.

00:01:49.930 --> 00:01:52.300
One of our big new
releases that I'm really

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:53.830
excited to talk
to you about today

00:01:53.830 --> 00:01:55.605
is the Dataproc Metastore.

00:01:55.605 --> 00:01:57.980
So I'm going to give you an
overview of what the Dataproc

00:01:57.980 --> 00:02:02.130
Metastore is and why we
have developed the Dataproc

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:04.250
Metastore.

00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:07.650
The Dataproc Metastore is,
essentially, a managed Apache

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:08.389
Hive metastore.

00:02:08.389 --> 00:02:12.140
So I want to start out with
why the Hive metastore is such

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:14.390
a big deal because
that drove why

00:02:14.390 --> 00:02:17.780
we developed a managed metastore
service in the first place.

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:20.150
The Apache Hive
metastore is really,

00:02:20.150 --> 00:02:24.590
as far as we see it, the
open standard for data lakes.

00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:27.500
There's a lot of
tooling that depends

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:32.100
on the Hive metastore, things
like Hive, Presto, Spark,

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:34.740
Impala, Hudi, and many more.

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:36.710
There's also an emerging
set of open source

00:02:36.710 --> 00:02:40.520
that uses the Hive metastore
in data lakes, things

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:43.600
like Iceberg and Delta Lake.

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:46.190
The Hive metastore integrates
with a lot of open source

00:02:46.190 --> 00:02:48.620
that people use as part
of a data lake, things

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:51.950
like Kerberos, Ranger, Atlas.

00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:54.650
And the metastore can
understand or represent

00:02:54.650 --> 00:02:57.530
data that's in many different
places, things like Hadoop

00:02:57.530 --> 00:03:01.640
clusters, cloud storage,
BigQuery, other cloud

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:05.130
providers, block storage,
Elasticsearch clusters, Kafka

00:03:05.130 --> 00:03:07.230
clusters, and so on.

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:10.070
So, to put it simply,
the Hive metastore

00:03:10.070 --> 00:03:13.280
is the central point
for open data lakes.

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:16.250
And it's a critical
path for any data

00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:20.580
lake, which is built, at least
partially, on open source.

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:22.820
So it's very important
that Hive metastore

00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:26.600
is usable and reliable.

00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:28.900
So we developed the
Dataproc Metastore.

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:32.770
We recently announced that it's
in alpha public availability.

00:03:32.770 --> 00:03:35.410
And the goal was to
have a fully managed,

00:03:35.410 --> 00:03:38.470
highly available, and
open-source native Apache Hive

00:03:38.470 --> 00:03:39.640
metastore.

00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:44.410
We want to provide an Apache
Hive metastore service that

00:03:44.410 --> 00:03:48.180
removes all of the barriers
for using both the Hive

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:51.910
metastore and the tooling
around the Hive metastore.

00:03:51.910 --> 00:03:55.120
From our point of view, if we
give you the best managed Spark

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:58.000
or Flink service, it
really falls apart

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:00.860
without a well-managed
Hive metastore,

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:03.970
which is why we developed
the Dataproc Metastore.

00:04:03.970 --> 00:04:05.867
It's also really
important-- and I'm

00:04:05.867 --> 00:04:07.450
going to underscore
this point again--

00:04:07.450 --> 00:04:11.990
that the Dataproc Metastore
is open-source native.

00:04:11.990 --> 00:04:16.630
We don't want to limit what
people can do with the Apache

00:04:16.630 --> 00:04:19.380
Hive metastore.

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:21.959
We want the Hive metastore
to be highly usable.

00:04:21.959 --> 00:04:24.100
And, often, Hive
metastores sound simple,

00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:26.550
but their actual
maintenance and operation

00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:28.650
can be quite complicated.

00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:31.140
Throughout our design of
the Dataproc Metastore,

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:33.630
we focused on
simplified management.

00:04:33.630 --> 00:04:37.650
This is an example of creating
a new Dataproc metastore.

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:39.810
In this case, you can
see the creation screen

00:04:39.810 --> 00:04:41.670
for just creating a metastore.

00:04:41.670 --> 00:04:44.760
You can see that we only require
a few simple options, things

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:47.130
like a name, the
metastore version, where

00:04:47.130 --> 00:04:49.020
you want to create
that metastore,

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:51.510
whether you want Kerberos
to be enabled or not.

00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:54.780
This makes getting started with
the Hive metastore very, very

00:04:54.780 --> 00:04:58.180
easy, essentially one click.

00:04:58.180 --> 00:05:00.280
Likewise, we want
the Hive metastore

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:02.210
to be very compatible.

00:05:02.210 --> 00:05:04.420
So, if you have an
existing Hive metastore,

00:05:04.420 --> 00:05:07.030
we want it to be very easy
for you to bring your data

00:05:07.030 --> 00:05:09.280
to the Dataproc Metastore.

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:12.460
In this example, we have
the import functionality.

00:05:12.460 --> 00:05:14.980
This allows you to take
an existing Hive metastore

00:05:14.980 --> 00:05:18.820
and import it into a
Dataproc metastore.

00:05:18.820 --> 00:05:21.598
So it should be easy to
create a new metastore

00:05:21.598 --> 00:05:23.890
and play around with the Hive
metastore and its tooling

00:05:23.890 --> 00:05:25.220
if you're a new user.

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:27.460
Or, if you're already
deeply embedded and invested

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:29.050
in the Hive metastore
ecosystem, it

00:05:29.050 --> 00:05:34.670
should be very easy to migrate
to use the Dataproc Metastore.

00:05:34.670 --> 00:05:37.330
Throughout the design and
implementation of the Dataproc

00:05:37.330 --> 00:05:39.440
Metastore, we've
wanted to create

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:43.550
a service, which is flexible,
integrated, and reliable.

00:05:43.550 --> 00:05:47.490
In terms of flexibility, as
you're using the Dataproc

00:05:47.490 --> 00:05:49.620
Metastore, if you
want to, for example,

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:52.940
scale your metastore up
because you have more users,

00:05:52.940 --> 00:05:56.800
or you have added more tooling,
which uses the Hive metastore,

00:05:56.800 --> 00:05:58.430
we want it to be
very easy for you

00:05:58.430 --> 00:06:02.810
to manage that metastore, the
resources, who can control,

00:06:02.810 --> 00:06:07.320
who has access to the metastore,
how it works, things like that.

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:09.740
We also want that
metastore to be integrated.

00:06:09.740 --> 00:06:12.290
As I mentioned previously,
the Hive metastore

00:06:12.290 --> 00:06:15.600
sits at the center of
an open-data ecosystem.

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:17.990
So it's really important
that the metastore

00:06:17.990 --> 00:06:21.080
works with a lot of
things out of the box.

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:24.050
As an example, we've worked
with Collibra and Qubole

00:06:24.050 --> 00:06:27.770
to make sure that they work out
of the box with the Dataproc

00:06:27.770 --> 00:06:28.820
Metastore.

00:06:28.820 --> 00:06:30.770
They're two examples,
but they showcase

00:06:30.770 --> 00:06:32.510
that we're very
focused on making sure

00:06:32.510 --> 00:06:37.420
that the Dataproc Metastore
is open and integrated.

00:06:37.420 --> 00:06:39.960
Metastores are also
very difficult to run

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:43.740
because they are a critical
path in your ecosystem.

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:47.370
If the metastore goes down,
often, many things break.

00:06:47.370 --> 00:06:51.477
So it's important to us that the
Dataproc Metastore is reliable.

00:06:51.477 --> 00:06:54.060
And, from the ground up, we've
designed the Dataproc Metastore

00:06:54.060 --> 00:06:55.455
so it's highly reliable.

00:06:59.020 --> 00:07:01.100
There's another really
interesting feature,

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:04.030
which I want to
showcase up front today,

00:07:04.030 --> 00:07:08.050
and that is Dataproc Hub.

00:07:08.050 --> 00:07:10.570
Let's start with two personas.

00:07:10.570 --> 00:07:13.690
On one hand, you have a data
scientist, and, on the other,

00:07:13.690 --> 00:07:15.790
you have an IT administrator.

00:07:15.790 --> 00:07:18.470
And say that both of
these people or personas

00:07:18.470 --> 00:07:21.820
work for the same organization.

00:07:21.820 --> 00:07:24.110
They have different needs.

00:07:24.110 --> 00:07:26.380
The data scientist wants
to create notebooks

00:07:26.380 --> 00:07:27.670
that are building blocks.

00:07:27.670 --> 00:07:29.050
They want to create models.

00:07:29.050 --> 00:07:32.140
They want to tinker and
experiment and explore

00:07:32.140 --> 00:07:33.160
with data.

00:07:33.160 --> 00:07:35.080
And, often, data
scientists want to do

00:07:35.080 --> 00:07:36.910
this in a way that's unimpeded.

00:07:36.910 --> 00:07:39.770
So they want to be able to
create a notebook and use

00:07:39.770 --> 00:07:40.660
tooling.

00:07:40.660 --> 00:07:43.120
And, often, data
scientists are preferential

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:45.700
to frameworks and tooling.

00:07:45.700 --> 00:07:49.450
So they just want to get
started and hands on with data.

00:07:49.450 --> 00:07:51.340
On the other, you
have IT administrators

00:07:51.340 --> 00:07:53.710
who are concerned about
things like making sure

00:07:53.710 --> 00:07:58.670
that code is production ready
and productionized properly.

00:07:58.670 --> 00:08:03.490
They also care about costs and
controls and security, things

00:08:03.490 --> 00:08:05.410
that the data
scientists might not

00:08:05.410 --> 00:08:08.410
have front and center
in mind when they want

00:08:08.410 --> 00:08:12.560
to get started with a notebook.

00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:15.310
What this usually means is that,
when a data scientist wants

00:08:15.310 --> 00:08:17.860
to create a notebook,
create a model,

00:08:17.860 --> 00:08:19.510
and then get something
in production,

00:08:19.510 --> 00:08:21.910
it can take weeks,
if not months,

00:08:21.910 --> 00:08:26.020
and require a lot
of steps in between.

00:08:26.020 --> 00:08:30.030
We think that things should
be much, much easier.

00:08:30.030 --> 00:08:34.150
The data scientist should
be able to create a notebook

00:08:34.150 --> 00:08:36.400
and then take that
notebook and have

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:38.470
it turn into
production-ready code

00:08:38.470 --> 00:08:43.730
or productionalize their
notebook as easily as possible.

00:08:43.730 --> 00:08:47.210
In essence, notebook should
be easy, configurable,

00:08:47.210 --> 00:08:49.170
and all in one place.

00:08:49.170 --> 00:08:50.587
There shouldn't
be a lot of steps.

00:08:50.587 --> 00:08:52.128
There shouldn't be
a lot of friction.

00:08:52.128 --> 00:08:54.590
And there shouldn't be a lot
of organizational frustration

00:08:54.590 --> 00:08:58.430
in trying to both enable
notebooks and also control them

00:08:58.430 --> 00:09:01.710
at the same time.

00:09:01.710 --> 00:09:03.320
So we built Dataproc Hub.

00:09:03.320 --> 00:09:06.200
We recently announced
Dataproc Hub in open beta.

00:09:06.200 --> 00:09:08.120
And Dataproc Hub is
built in conjunction

00:09:08.120 --> 00:09:11.690
with the AI Platform Notebooks
functionality on Google Cloud

00:09:11.690 --> 00:09:13.990
platform.

00:09:13.990 --> 00:09:15.610
Ultimately, what
this means is that we

00:09:15.610 --> 00:09:19.190
are bringing data scientists
and IT administrators together.

00:09:19.190 --> 00:09:21.460
And we're also bringing
notebooks together

00:09:21.460 --> 00:09:25.120
with AI Platform
tools and Dataproc.

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:29.170
As a result, data scientists
can easily create notebooks.

00:09:29.170 --> 00:09:32.230
They can choose their
favorite open-source tool kits

00:09:32.230 --> 00:09:33.747
and frameworks.

00:09:33.747 --> 00:09:35.830
And they can use advanced
features on Google Cloud

00:09:35.830 --> 00:09:40.970
platform on Dataproc clusters,
things like GPUs or SSDs

00:09:40.970 --> 00:09:45.058
if they have workloads
that are IOPS performant.

00:09:45.058 --> 00:09:46.600
What this means for
IT administrators

00:09:46.600 --> 00:09:50.330
is that they can predefine who
can do what with notebooks,

00:09:50.330 --> 00:09:53.110
what the size and shape
of Dataproc clusters

00:09:53.110 --> 00:09:55.510
looks like, what features
are automatically enabled

00:09:55.510 --> 00:09:56.860
or disallowed.

00:09:56.860 --> 00:09:59.350
It also makes it very easy
for these IT administrators

00:09:59.350 --> 00:10:01.750
to see exactly who
is using notebooks

00:10:01.750 --> 00:10:04.550
and then take what's being done
and productionalize it very,

00:10:04.550 --> 00:10:07.530
very easily.

00:10:07.530 --> 00:10:10.290
The IT administrator story
is really important to us,

00:10:10.290 --> 00:10:12.900
as we think about notebook use.

00:10:12.900 --> 00:10:14.610
And, as the first--

00:10:14.610 --> 00:10:16.470
it's optional, but,
as a first step,

00:10:16.470 --> 00:10:18.930
an IT administrator
can predefine who

00:10:18.930 --> 00:10:21.060
can do what with Dataproc Hub.

00:10:21.060 --> 00:10:23.430
Specifically, what this
means is an IT administrator

00:10:23.430 --> 00:10:27.150
can predefine what size,
shape, location, and features

00:10:27.150 --> 00:10:28.980
are used in a
Dataproc cluster that

00:10:28.980 --> 00:10:32.520
powers a Dataproc Hub notebook.

00:10:32.520 --> 00:10:35.280
That way, once somebody gets
their Jupyter-based Dataproc

00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:37.920
Hub notebook, all
of the constraints

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:40.080
are automatically applied
for the cluster that runs

00:10:40.080 --> 00:10:41.880
and powers that notebook.

00:10:41.880 --> 00:10:43.950
In this example, we have
a YAML-based cluster

00:10:43.950 --> 00:10:47.772
configuration that specifies
an idle configuration.

00:10:47.772 --> 00:10:49.230
So, in this case,
the cluster would

00:10:49.230 --> 00:10:53.340
be deleted if it was idle
for more than 7,200 seconds.

00:10:53.340 --> 00:10:56.520
An IT administrator can
use this configuration

00:10:56.520 --> 00:11:00.960
as a template for clusters that
are then used for Dataproc Hub

00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:01.890
notebooks.

00:11:01.890 --> 00:11:05.382
So, if a cluster is idle, it
automatically gets deleted.

00:11:05.382 --> 00:11:06.840
And this showcases
how we're trying

00:11:06.840 --> 00:11:10.230
to bring both data scientists
and IT administrators together

00:11:10.230 --> 00:11:13.070
with Dataproc Hub.

00:11:13.070 --> 00:11:15.700
Ultimately, the flow
of using a notebook

00:11:15.700 --> 00:11:18.040
with Dataproc Hub and
AI Platform Notebooks

00:11:18.040 --> 00:11:20.720
should be fairly easy.

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:24.840
The data scientist can go
look for examples or snippets

00:11:24.840 --> 00:11:28.900
in AI Notebooks Platform or
go directly to Dataproc Hub

00:11:28.900 --> 00:11:33.100
to create new notebooks or
reuse existing notebooks.

00:11:33.100 --> 00:11:37.240
Then they can use that notebook
and Dataproc or BigQuery

00:11:37.240 --> 00:11:42.910
to prepare, process, and
create models, data sets.

00:11:42.910 --> 00:11:45.040
All the while, the
IT administrator

00:11:45.040 --> 00:11:47.710
can see who's doing
what with notebooks.

00:11:47.710 --> 00:11:49.990
They can preconfigure
the boundaries

00:11:49.990 --> 00:11:53.352
on what notebooks can do in the
clusters that underlie them.

00:11:53.352 --> 00:11:55.060
And then they can do
things like schedule

00:11:55.060 --> 00:11:56.920
and manage notebooks
that have already

00:11:56.920 --> 00:11:59.740
been created by Dataproc Hub.

00:11:59.740 --> 00:12:02.620
Ultimately, this is
showcasing that it

00:12:02.620 --> 00:12:06.160
can be a frictionless process
between data scientists and IT

00:12:06.160 --> 00:12:07.420
administrators.

00:12:07.420 --> 00:12:11.050
It also makes it very easy
to create Dataproc clusters

00:12:11.050 --> 00:12:15.100
with Jupyter-based Dataproc Hub
notebooks for data scientists.

00:12:15.100 --> 00:12:17.680
So there's fewer hoops
to jump through when

00:12:17.680 --> 00:12:19.490
you want to do data science.

00:12:19.490 --> 00:12:21.370
So, in this case,
I'm going to show you

00:12:21.370 --> 00:12:24.310
what a quick demonstration
of Dataproc Hub

00:12:24.310 --> 00:12:27.430
looks like to show you why
it's really easy to get

00:12:27.430 --> 00:12:30.010
started with Dataproc
Hub to do data

00:12:30.010 --> 00:12:32.240
science with open-source tools.

00:12:32.240 --> 00:12:36.040
So, in this example,
I am a data scientist,

00:12:36.040 --> 00:12:40.390
and I want to maybe
use Spark and BigQuery.

00:12:40.390 --> 00:12:44.560
So I know I can go to AI
Hub and find some examples

00:12:44.560 --> 00:12:46.660
for common situations.

00:12:46.660 --> 00:12:48.550
In this case, I'm
going to see if there's

00:12:48.550 --> 00:12:52.900
an example for how to use
Spark with BigQuery together.

00:12:52.900 --> 00:12:56.110
So I search for
BigQuery here, and I

00:12:56.110 --> 00:12:57.820
will get a list of results.

00:12:57.820 --> 00:13:00.250
And, in the results, I can
see that there is indeed

00:13:00.250 --> 00:13:03.940
a quick example showing how
to use Spark with BigQuery.

00:13:03.940 --> 00:13:05.920
And this is using the
Spark-BigQuery connector

00:13:05.920 --> 00:13:07.870
that's provided on Dataproc.

00:13:07.870 --> 00:13:11.110
I then can take that
example and open it

00:13:11.110 --> 00:13:13.790
in the AI Platform Notebooks.

00:13:13.790 --> 00:13:15.760
And I already have a
preconfigured workbench

00:13:15.760 --> 00:13:19.370
that an IT administrator
has preconfigured for me.

00:13:19.370 --> 00:13:20.300
I then sign in.

00:13:20.300 --> 00:13:22.650
So Dataproc Hub does
perform the authorization

00:13:22.650 --> 00:13:24.340
and authentication checks.

00:13:24.340 --> 00:13:27.190
And you can see that the cluster
that is behind this notebook

00:13:27.190 --> 00:13:30.250
has been already preconfigured
by the IT administrator.

00:13:30.250 --> 00:13:33.730
So, as a data scientist, all I
need to do is just click Start.

00:13:33.730 --> 00:13:35.840
This creates the notebook.

00:13:35.840 --> 00:13:38.170
It also creates the
cluster that powers

00:13:38.170 --> 00:13:40.370
the notebook behind the scenes.

00:13:40.370 --> 00:13:43.090
You can see in this example
that all the data scientist had

00:13:43.090 --> 00:13:45.880
to do was click
Start, and they got

00:13:45.880 --> 00:13:47.485
the notebook and the cluster.

00:13:47.485 --> 00:13:49.360
They don't have to think
about configuration.

00:13:49.360 --> 00:13:51.902
They don't have to think about
networking plumbing or cluster

00:13:51.902 --> 00:13:52.940
options.

00:13:52.940 --> 00:13:55.000
The data scientist
doesn't even necessarily

00:13:55.000 --> 00:13:56.740
have to know what Dataproc is.

00:13:56.740 --> 00:13:59.200
All they did was create a
notebook and get started.

00:14:02.200 --> 00:14:03.970
Beyond Dataproc
Hub, I want to talk

00:14:03.970 --> 00:14:07.540
through some of the really
important open-source updates

00:14:07.540 --> 00:14:11.560
that we've provided on Dataproc
in the last several months.

00:14:11.560 --> 00:14:14.290
We make it a point to
keep up with the latest

00:14:14.290 --> 00:14:16.160
and greatest in open source.

00:14:16.160 --> 00:14:18.550
And we're really
passionate about bringing

00:14:18.550 --> 00:14:22.000
new open-source developments
to you in Dataproc as a managed

00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:23.560
service.

00:14:23.560 --> 00:14:26.260
One of the big advancements
that I want to showcase

00:14:26.260 --> 00:14:30.760
is Dataproc 2, which just
entered public preview.

00:14:30.760 --> 00:14:34.030
Dataproc 2 includes some
significant upgrades

00:14:34.030 --> 00:14:35.590
in the default
components that we

00:14:35.590 --> 00:14:38.530
install on Dataproc clusters.

00:14:38.530 --> 00:14:43.240
What this means is you get
Hadoop 3, Spark 3, Hive 3.1,

00:14:43.240 --> 00:14:46.810
Java 11, and several other
upgrades to components

00:14:46.810 --> 00:14:49.180
that we put on
clusters by default.

00:14:49.180 --> 00:14:52.270
We keep the number of components
that we install by default

00:14:52.270 --> 00:14:53.740
somewhat slimmed down.

00:14:53.740 --> 00:14:55.430
So you have efficient clusters.

00:14:55.430 --> 00:14:59.870
And we narrow the installs down
to the most-used components,

00:14:59.870 --> 00:15:02.500
but there's still a
whole set of things

00:15:02.500 --> 00:15:04.450
you can install on
Dataproc clusters

00:15:04.450 --> 00:15:06.790
through initialization
actions, custom

00:15:06.790 --> 00:15:10.450
images, optional
components, and other means.

00:15:10.450 --> 00:15:11.920
These optional
installs have also

00:15:11.920 --> 00:15:14.350
been updated in
Dataproc image 2.

00:15:14.350 --> 00:15:17.410
So it includes things
like Apache Flink,

00:15:17.410 --> 00:15:22.240
updates to Apache HBase,
Druid, Presto, and others.

00:15:22.240 --> 00:15:25.120
So Dataproc 2 is a
brand new image line

00:15:25.120 --> 00:15:28.420
in Dataproc designed to bring
the Hadoop 3 and Spark 3

00:15:28.420 --> 00:15:31.860
ecosystem to you.

00:15:31.860 --> 00:15:34.130
I specifically want
to talk about Spark 3

00:15:34.130 --> 00:15:36.980
because Spark 3 includes
a number of really

00:15:36.980 --> 00:15:38.630
interesting and
important updates

00:15:38.630 --> 00:15:41.180
that we're excited both
to bring you in Dataproc,

00:15:41.180 --> 00:15:43.340
and we're very
interested in seeing

00:15:43.340 --> 00:15:45.650
how we can bring
these functionalities

00:15:45.650 --> 00:15:48.780
and improvements to
future Dataproc features.

00:15:48.780 --> 00:15:52.100
So the theme for Spark
3 was performance,

00:15:52.100 --> 00:15:54.620
and there's three
good examples of what

00:15:54.620 --> 00:15:59.060
that means for you in terms
of performance in Spark 3.

00:15:59.060 --> 00:16:02.270
First, Spark 3 includes
better adaptive query support.

00:16:02.270 --> 00:16:04.760
So Spark can
optimize a query plan

00:16:04.760 --> 00:16:07.320
while things are executing.

00:16:07.320 --> 00:16:09.920
Spark 3 also has dynamic
partition pruning.

00:16:09.920 --> 00:16:14.330
So unnecessary scans
are avoided in queries

00:16:14.330 --> 00:16:16.910
that could look like
data warehouse queries

00:16:16.910 --> 00:16:19.350
so, again, faster querying.

00:16:19.350 --> 00:16:23.370
Spark 3 also has
improved GPU support,

00:16:23.370 --> 00:16:24.900
and we're really
excited because we

00:16:24.900 --> 00:16:28.380
think there's a future where
Spark 3 can run entirely

00:16:28.380 --> 00:16:32.160
on GPUs, both for data
science and, potentially, just

00:16:32.160 --> 00:16:33.700
general data processing.

00:16:33.700 --> 00:16:36.330
So we're very interested in
seeing what the future holds

00:16:36.330 --> 00:16:39.640
for Spark and GPUs.

00:16:39.640 --> 00:16:42.040
There's also a lot of
advancements for Spark

00:16:42.040 --> 00:16:44.410
running on Kubernetes.

00:16:44.410 --> 00:16:46.120
I'll talk about
it in just a bit,

00:16:46.120 --> 00:16:48.790
but Dataproc has a
flavor, which allows

00:16:48.790 --> 00:16:50.890
you to run Spark on
Kubernetes clusters

00:16:50.890 --> 00:16:52.600
that are managed by Dataproc.

00:16:52.600 --> 00:16:55.690
So we're very excited to see
new advancements in Kubernetes

00:16:55.690 --> 00:16:59.740
and Spark because it will
only make both the Spark

00:16:59.740 --> 00:17:03.040
community better, but also
makes Dataproc a much more

00:17:03.040 --> 00:17:07.290
interesting product from
a Kubernetes perspective.

00:17:07.290 --> 00:17:10.170
I mentioned GPUs
before, but this

00:17:10.170 --> 00:17:12.910
is something that's really,
really exciting to us.

00:17:12.910 --> 00:17:14.730
And we've worked
closely with NVIDIA

00:17:14.730 --> 00:17:18.240
to make sure that GPUs
work well in Spark

00:17:18.240 --> 00:17:23.859
and also that GPUs work well
on GCP and Dataproc clusters.

00:17:23.859 --> 00:17:26.940
So you can create Dataproc
clusters with NVIDIA GPUs

00:17:26.940 --> 00:17:28.240
today.

00:17:28.240 --> 00:17:30.890
We've also worked with NVIDIA
on things like the RAPIDS

00:17:30.890 --> 00:17:34.080
frameworks to make sure
that you can do really

00:17:34.080 --> 00:17:36.930
high-performance data
processing and data

00:17:36.930 --> 00:17:39.330
science on Dataproc clusters.

00:17:39.330 --> 00:17:42.990
If you're interested in
using Spark 3 with GPUs,

00:17:42.990 --> 00:17:45.060
I would highly
encourage you to look

00:17:45.060 --> 00:17:48.060
at the documentation on
the Dataproc site and also

00:17:48.060 --> 00:17:49.980
the RAPIDS framework.

00:17:49.980 --> 00:17:52.890
We think this is a really
interesting avenue for Spark

00:17:52.890 --> 00:17:57.300
in open source in the
long term to run on GPUs.

00:17:57.300 --> 00:18:01.020
It's very easy to get started
with Dataproc 2 today.

00:18:01.020 --> 00:18:02.910
All you need to do is
specify that you want

00:18:02.910 --> 00:18:05.790
to run the preview of Dataproc.

00:18:05.790 --> 00:18:08.220
So this is an example
of a Cloud SDK command

00:18:08.220 --> 00:18:11.730
or a gcloud that specifies
the preview image.

00:18:11.730 --> 00:18:13.380
I also want to call
out how easy it

00:18:13.380 --> 00:18:16.140
is to use different sets
of open-source components

00:18:16.140 --> 00:18:17.670
on Dataproc clusters.

00:18:17.670 --> 00:18:19.500
You don't have to
think about upgrading

00:18:19.500 --> 00:18:23.370
one whole cluster to new
versions of Spark or Hadoop.

00:18:23.370 --> 00:18:25.530
If you want to try out
a new version of Spark,

00:18:25.530 --> 00:18:28.920
in this case, Spark 3, create
a very small new cluster

00:18:28.920 --> 00:18:30.190
and use the preview image.

00:18:30.190 --> 00:18:31.350
Try a job out.

00:18:31.350 --> 00:18:33.210
Try some of the
new features out.

00:18:33.210 --> 00:18:35.490
Then you can delete the
cluster when you're done.

00:18:35.490 --> 00:18:37.380
Dataproc imaging is
a subtle feature,

00:18:37.380 --> 00:18:40.380
but makes it very
easy and interesting

00:18:40.380 --> 00:18:42.390
to try out different
sets and versions

00:18:42.390 --> 00:18:44.820
of open-source
components without fear

00:18:44.820 --> 00:18:46.530
of breaking clusters
that are running

00:18:46.530 --> 00:18:50.250
production or high-value jobs.

00:18:50.250 --> 00:18:53.310
There's also a whole set
of new Dataproc features

00:18:53.310 --> 00:18:57.690
that I want to run
you through as well.

00:18:57.690 --> 00:19:00.150
As I mentioned, we have
a flavor of Dataproc,

00:19:00.150 --> 00:19:02.640
which runs on Kubernetes.

00:19:02.640 --> 00:19:06.030
We've recently announced
Dataproc on Kubernetes Engine

00:19:06.030 --> 00:19:08.320
clusters in beta.

00:19:08.320 --> 00:19:10.920
What this means is that
you can run Spark jobs

00:19:10.920 --> 00:19:15.090
with Dataproc on GKE clusters.

00:19:15.090 --> 00:19:18.840
We are really excited about the
future of running open source

00:19:18.840 --> 00:19:20.820
on Kubernetes clusters.

00:19:20.820 --> 00:19:23.040
I'll give you three
examples of why.

00:19:23.040 --> 00:19:26.580
One, we think that it allows
better resource management.

00:19:26.580 --> 00:19:29.280
So you can have multiple
things running on one cluster

00:19:29.280 --> 00:19:32.550
and be a little bit more
sure that resources are being

00:19:32.550 --> 00:19:34.380
balanced and managed properly.

00:19:34.380 --> 00:19:36.630
It also allows you to take
one Kubernetes cluster

00:19:36.630 --> 00:19:38.700
and run open-source
data processing

00:19:38.700 --> 00:19:40.690
with something like
a web application

00:19:40.690 --> 00:19:42.420
if you were so inclined.

00:19:42.420 --> 00:19:45.240
It also potentially allows
for better job isolation.

00:19:45.240 --> 00:19:48.540
And, as customers get
better resource management,

00:19:48.540 --> 00:19:52.420
job isolation is something
that goes hand in hand.

00:19:52.420 --> 00:19:56.580
We're also really interested
in both seeing and helping

00:19:56.580 --> 00:19:59.820
these open-source
frameworks and tool kits

00:19:59.820 --> 00:20:02.100
work with some of the more
advanced and interesting

00:20:02.100 --> 00:20:04.500
features on Kubernetes,
things like auto scaling,

00:20:04.500 --> 00:20:06.960
node healing, and the
like, because that

00:20:06.960 --> 00:20:10.210
allows for more dynamic
clusters over time.

00:20:10.210 --> 00:20:13.050
The Dataproc has been involved
in getting both Spark and Flink

00:20:13.050 --> 00:20:15.390
working on Kubernetes.

00:20:15.390 --> 00:20:17.160
Last year, we released
both an operator

00:20:17.160 --> 00:20:20.040
for Spark and Kubernetes.

00:20:20.040 --> 00:20:21.480
Because it's a
common question, I

00:20:21.480 --> 00:20:24.750
do want to call out that
we will support both YARN

00:20:24.750 --> 00:20:27.580
and Kubernetes well
into the future of data.

00:20:27.580 --> 00:20:29.580
We're not making a
wholesale decision

00:20:29.580 --> 00:20:31.410
to switch from one to the other.

00:20:31.410 --> 00:20:33.630
We see customers over
the next several years

00:20:33.630 --> 00:20:36.370
will want to use both
for different reasons,

00:20:36.370 --> 00:20:39.780
and we have a plan to support
both YARN and Kubernetes well

00:20:39.780 --> 00:20:42.700
into the future.

00:20:42.700 --> 00:20:45.060
Another feature that I'm
really excited to mention

00:20:45.060 --> 00:20:47.670
is Dataproc flex mode.

00:20:47.670 --> 00:20:51.570
I'll explain flex mode through
a problem with both scaling

00:20:51.570 --> 00:20:53.310
and virtual machines.

00:20:53.310 --> 00:20:55.230
When you're trying
to scale down,

00:20:55.230 --> 00:20:58.110
you may hold on to a
node or a set of nodes

00:20:58.110 --> 00:20:59.970
because they have
intermediate data.

00:20:59.970 --> 00:21:01.200
They finished processing.

00:21:01.200 --> 00:21:02.700
They're just holding
on to something

00:21:02.700 --> 00:21:04.658
that they can't get rid
of, but, otherwise, you

00:21:04.658 --> 00:21:06.270
would get rid of the node.

00:21:06.270 --> 00:21:08.100
Likewise, with
preemptibles, you may

00:21:08.100 --> 00:21:10.650
have a node that's finished
processing or reached

00:21:10.650 --> 00:21:15.180
a state where it can checkpoint,
but the node is preempted.

00:21:15.180 --> 00:21:17.130
In both cases, if
you removed the node,

00:21:17.130 --> 00:21:18.620
you would lose the data.

00:21:18.620 --> 00:21:21.160
Flex mode is a
stateful data store

00:21:21.160 --> 00:21:23.520
that allows you to
store stateful data,

00:21:23.520 --> 00:21:27.820
such as MapReduce shuffle
data, in HDFS on the cluster.

00:21:27.820 --> 00:21:29.430
This makes clusters
more flexible

00:21:29.430 --> 00:21:33.750
because you can add and remove
clusters much more easily

00:21:33.750 --> 00:21:35.580
without interrupting your job.

00:21:35.580 --> 00:21:38.340
So, in my example, if
you wanted to scale down,

00:21:38.340 --> 00:21:41.410
once the node is done computing,
it can put all of its data

00:21:41.410 --> 00:21:46.080
in flex pool, and then the node
can simply be auto scaled down.

00:21:46.080 --> 00:21:48.360
Likewise, if you lose
a preemptible VM that's

00:21:48.360 --> 00:21:51.090
finished processing,
that preemptible VM

00:21:51.090 --> 00:21:53.500
has already put its
data in the flex pool.

00:21:53.500 --> 00:21:56.760
So its preemption presents
no risk to your job.

00:21:56.760 --> 00:21:59.310
Flex mode should allow
better auto scaling

00:21:59.310 --> 00:22:01.750
and also should allow you
to use more preemptibles

00:22:01.750 --> 00:22:03.450
in your Dataproc
clusters, which,

00:22:03.450 --> 00:22:08.180
again, means greater flexibility
and cost optimization.

00:22:08.180 --> 00:22:09.770
There's two new
security features,

00:22:09.770 --> 00:22:13.510
which we're adding to the
pile of pre-existing Dataproc

00:22:13.510 --> 00:22:15.080
security features.

00:22:15.080 --> 00:22:18.450
So Dataproc already allows
you to use security features,

00:22:18.450 --> 00:22:21.920
such as Ranger, customer-managed
and customer-supplied

00:22:21.920 --> 00:22:27.950
encryption keys, Kerberos, VPC
Service Controls, our component

00:22:27.950 --> 00:22:29.600
gateway, which
allows you to access

00:22:29.600 --> 00:22:33.800
web UIs with no networking, but
still provides authorization.

00:22:33.800 --> 00:22:35.360
All of these are
great, but customers

00:22:35.360 --> 00:22:37.440
have told us they
want more security,

00:22:37.440 --> 00:22:39.380
which we've been
squarely focused on.

00:22:39.380 --> 00:22:42.050
And these are two of multiple
new security features,

00:22:42.050 --> 00:22:44.390
which you'll see in the future.

00:22:44.390 --> 00:22:46.820
One of the security features
is secure extensions

00:22:46.820 --> 00:22:48.080
for notebooks.

00:22:48.080 --> 00:22:50.750
What this means is that
you can give somebody

00:22:50.750 --> 00:22:54.220
a notebook, and that someone
or that data scientist,

00:22:54.220 --> 00:22:56.300
for example, their
credentials will

00:22:56.300 --> 00:23:00.500
be used for execution of
the job and to communicate

00:23:00.500 --> 00:23:03.930
with whatever services
that job connects with.

00:23:03.930 --> 00:23:06.470
So, for example, if I
am a data scientist,

00:23:06.470 --> 00:23:09.080
and I interact
with Cloud Storage,

00:23:09.080 --> 00:23:11.465
my data scientist
individual credentials

00:23:11.465 --> 00:23:13.850
will be used to go
talk to Cloud Storage,

00:23:13.850 --> 00:23:15.520
instead of a service account.

00:23:15.520 --> 00:23:19.070
And this brings better
per-user isolation and security

00:23:19.070 --> 00:23:21.610
to notebooks.

00:23:21.610 --> 00:23:23.900
A second feature that
we recently announced

00:23:23.900 --> 00:23:25.220
was Shielded VMs.

00:23:25.220 --> 00:23:27.110
So, if you're using
Dataproc clusters

00:23:27.110 --> 00:23:33.890
with Ubuntu 18 or Debian 10,
you can use Shielded VMs.

00:23:33.890 --> 00:23:38.420
Again, these are two new out
of future security improvements

00:23:38.420 --> 00:23:41.330
that you'll continue to see
in Dataproc to make sure

00:23:41.330 --> 00:23:43.430
that customers have all
of the security features

00:23:43.430 --> 00:23:47.480
both that they might want, but,
also, that we could potentially

00:23:47.480 --> 00:23:49.130
offer as a cloud provider.

00:23:49.130 --> 00:23:51.830
I want to finally
mention something

00:23:51.830 --> 00:23:53.370
that doesn't get a
lot of attention,

00:23:53.370 --> 00:23:55.870
but is kind of a continual
feature in Dataproc.

00:23:55.870 --> 00:23:58.760
And that's additional
compute flexibility.

00:23:58.760 --> 00:24:01.250
Every time there's a
new region or a new VM

00:24:01.250 --> 00:24:04.610
type or a new
accelerator, we try

00:24:04.610 --> 00:24:06.860
to wire those
through to Dataproc

00:24:06.860 --> 00:24:08.760
as quickly as possible.

00:24:08.760 --> 00:24:12.140
What this means is you get
new levers for cost and speed

00:24:12.140 --> 00:24:13.550
optimization.

00:24:13.550 --> 00:24:15.770
In this example, say that
you had a workload, which

00:24:15.770 --> 00:24:17.510
was performance
insensitive, and you just

00:24:17.510 --> 00:24:19.340
wanted to lower your cost.

00:24:19.340 --> 00:24:23.360
You could use E2 machine types
to lower your cost relative

00:24:23.360 --> 00:24:26.510
to maybe you were running
on N1 standard forms.

00:24:26.510 --> 00:24:28.750
You can see on the table
all of the VMs kind of look

00:24:28.750 --> 00:24:31.110
and feel the same, but
the cost is different.

00:24:31.110 --> 00:24:33.620
This is an example of how
additional compute flexibility

00:24:33.620 --> 00:24:36.590
can give you some really
powerful levers to speed things

00:24:36.590 --> 00:24:41.240
up or to optimize cost,
depending on your needs.

00:24:41.240 --> 00:24:43.100
Finally, I want to
talk about how you

00:24:43.100 --> 00:24:46.438
can get started with Dataproc.

00:24:46.438 --> 00:24:48.230
The best place to get
started with Dataproc

00:24:48.230 --> 00:24:51.360
is our documentation on
the Google Cloud site.

00:24:51.360 --> 00:24:55.010
We also have a set of tutorials
on the Dataproc site, things

00:24:55.010 --> 00:24:57.980
like using Spark with
BigQuery or creating clusters

00:24:57.980 --> 00:24:59.660
to run Presto.

00:24:59.660 --> 00:25:02.030
So you should be able to
run through those tutorials

00:25:02.030 --> 00:25:04.520
to understand Dataproc and
some of the open-source tooling

00:25:04.520 --> 00:25:05.810
pretty quickly.

00:25:05.810 --> 00:25:07.370
If you're brand new
to Google Cloud,

00:25:07.370 --> 00:25:08.930
I really encourage
you to take a look

00:25:08.930 --> 00:25:12.350
at the free trial for
Google Cloud Platform

00:25:12.350 --> 00:25:16.015
to get started with both
Google Cloud and also Dataproc.

00:25:16.015 --> 00:25:17.765
If you're curious about
Dataproc or you're

00:25:17.765 --> 00:25:20.360
an existing Dataproc
customer and want

00:25:20.360 --> 00:25:25.250
to know more or participate,
we have a Stack Overflow tag,

00:25:25.250 --> 00:25:27.080
google-cloud-dataproc,
which you can

00:25:27.080 --> 00:25:29.830
use to find questions
and ask new questions.

00:25:29.830 --> 00:25:31.580
Often, you'll see the
Dataproc engineering

00:25:31.580 --> 00:25:36.180
team trying to help customers
and Stack Overflow users.

00:25:36.180 --> 00:25:39.650
We also have a Google Group,
cloud-dataproc-discuss,

00:25:39.650 --> 00:25:41.570
and a feedback email list.

00:25:41.570 --> 00:25:45.440
And please do send us your
feedback and thoughts.

00:25:45.440 --> 00:25:48.050
It's customers that
largely influence and help

00:25:48.050 --> 00:25:49.110
drive our road map.

00:25:49.110 --> 00:25:52.970
So we are passionate on hearing
and responding to our customers

00:25:52.970 --> 00:25:55.508
and whatever needs you may have.

00:25:55.508 --> 00:25:57.800
With that, I want to thank
you very much for your time,

00:25:57.800 --> 00:25:59.710
and have a great day.

